


Jonah whispered toward the phone. “Do that again.”




Maren turned back to him. “Stop prompting.”




A brief sound came from behind her.




She swung the camera toward the rise. The image blurred through branches before autofocus caught the nearest trunk. Beyond it, brush bent and returned in a place her camera light did not fully reach.




Jonah stood too quickly. His knee struck the stone, and the phone shifted half an inch across the surface.




The recording continued.




“Stay still,” he said.




Maren held the camera on the brush. “I am still.”




A third disturbance seemed to come from the opposite side of the stone: several quick leaf contacts, then one heavier sound close to the ground. The sound could have come from an animal circling the light, from more than one animal, or from their earlier passage releasing vegetation on the slope. The pauses made Maren imagine one source moving around the stone, though she had heard three separate disturbances.




Jonah raised the handheld recorder. “It’s coming from both sides.”




Maren kept the camera on the brush.




Jonah kept the recorder raised.




She did not lower the camera.




They were both breathing hard.




Maren backed until her shoulder touched a tree. From there she could hold Jonah, the phone, and part of the brush in one frame. The stone lay between them. Jonah’s face occupied the upper right of the image. The phone screen glowed at the bottom. The recorder timer continued to advance.




“Are you the one that sent me away?” he asked.




Maren lowered the camera. “Jonah.”




He spoke past her. “Did you want me back here alone?”




“Stop.”




Maren heard a short sound. It contained enough variation to resemble a voice at first contact, but no syllable remained when Maren listened for it. The leaves around them had not stirred before the sound. The insects continued.




Jonah looked at her. “You heard that.”




Maren kept the camera on him.




Jonah waited.




She did not answer.




He turned toward the phone. “Say it again.”




Maren saw the tally light reflected as a red point in his eyes.




Jonah had placed himself beside the stone, turned off his light, and begun supplying questions for the file. Maren had come to get him out. Instead, she was still filming while he stayed crouched beside the phone and waited for the next noise.




She pressed the record button.




The tally light went out. The camera light remained on.




Jonah stared at the lens. “Why did you stop?”




“Because we are done.”




“Turn it back on.”




“No.”




“You heard it.”




Maren clipped the camera strap across her body and moved around the near end of the stone. Jonah shifted between her and the phone.




“Move,” she said.




“One more minute.”




“No.”




“It came when I asked it to come closer. Then you came up the hill.”




Maren did not answer.




“And it kept moving after you stopped.”




A narrow line of leaves flattened behind the stone, then lifted.




Maren held the light on the area. A branch moved once at knee height. Past the trunk, she saw nothing else move.




Jonah reached toward the phone.




Maren caught his bandaged right hand before he could brace it on the stone. He made a sharp sound and tried to pull back. She loosened her grip but did not release him.




“I’m holding your hand,” she said. “Stand up.”




“The phone.”




“Leave it.”




“It’s the only clean recording.”




“You are bleeding. We are off trail. You are not answering the radio. Stand up.”




He planted his left hand on his knee and rose. His balance failed at the top of the movement. Maren stepped backward and took his weight against her shoulder. The bandaged hand closed hard around hers.




Another disturbance sounded in the brush behind him.




This one came in two stages: a close crack, then several contacts from lower on the slope. Jonah twisted to look. Maren pulled his hand toward the route.




“Do not follow it.”




“I’m not.”




He took one step with her, then stopped. “My phone.”




The screen remained visible on the stone. The red waveform advanced. The phone sat three steps back around the exposed end, toward the brush where the branch had moved. Maren could release Jonah, take the phone, and preserve the file. She could also keep hold of him and leave immediately.




A low impact sounded from the far side of the stone.




Jonah flinched into her.




Maren pulled him downhill.




They moved without a path. Leaves slid beneath their boots. Jonah’s injured hand stayed locked around hers while his left arm pushed branches away from his face. Maren kept the camera light ahead but did not restart the recording. Behind them, the phone’s screen disappeared whenever a trunk crossed the line of sight, returned once as a small white bar, and then vanished below the rise.




“Which way?” Jonah asked.




“Down to the white route.”




“That sound was above us.”




“Keep moving.”




A branch caught his jacket. He tore free and nearly fell. Maren turned sideways, braced him against a tree, and waited until both feet were under him.




Maren heard no movement behind them during the pause.




The river became audible again.




Maren aimed the light downslope. The white triangle at marker four appeared between two trunks, brighter than anything else in the woods.




“There.”




They reached the trail together.




Maren pulled Jonah onto the packed ground and stopped. He stood beside her, bent at the waist, still holding her hand. Her camera hung against her hip. Both of their lights faced the slope they had left.




Neither of them moved.




From the direction of the stone came one slow, dull contact.




Neither of them went back for the phone.




Scene 8 — After They Left




The sound from the slope did not repeat.




Maren waited with Jonah at marker four until she could again hear the river below the trail and insects close around them. His hand stayed inside hers. The new blood had spread through the gauze at his palm but had not reached his wrist. When he straightened, his knees held.




Her camera remained off.




She checked the display anyway. 11:56.




“White marker four,” she said into the radio. “We are together. We are returning to the shelter and exiting. We left one phone off trail. No one goes back for it tonight.”




The transmission reached only Jonah’s radio. To reach Lena, she needed her phone, and she had both hands occupied until he stopped leaning against her.




“Can you walk?” she asked.




“Yes.”




“Show me.”




He took four steps along the packed trail. His right hand remained against his chest. He did not look upslope.




Maren released him and called Lena.




Lena answered before the second ring. “You’re early.”




“We’re ending now.” Maren turned away from Jonah and kept her voice level. “We separated. Jonah left the marked route. I followed him. We’re both back on white at marker four. He reopened his hand. We left his phone off trail because I wasn’t taking him back for it.”




A pause.




“Do I call Ruth?” Lena asked.




“Yes. Tell her we’re coming out and that we need the phone recovered in daylight. I’ll call her when we reach the shelter.”
